WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Hardening changes can affect production systems, so they need controlled rollout.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:10.020
A change may remove software, adjust local permissions, modify remote access,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.040
enable endpoint protection features, change logging, or apply a new baseline.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.700
Even defensive changes can break workflows if they are pushed without testing.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:24.440
Use maintenance windows, peer review, pilot groups, staged deployment, backups,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.980
and rollback plans when the risk warrants it.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.960
Validation confirms that controls are applied and still working.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:36.860
After a change, verify expected configuration, endpoint agent health, log delivery,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.760
application function, user experience, backup status, and any monitoring signals.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.700
Validation should be specific enough to catch failure,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.720
not just a quick statement that the change was completed.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.900
Evidence matters because configuration drift is common.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.280
Exceptions are sometimes necessary.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.780
A legacy application may require an older package.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.520
A specialized device may not support the preferred endpoint agent.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.580
A production system may need a delayed patch because of vendor dependency.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.920
Exceptions should have owners, reasons, compensating controls, expiration dates,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.660
and review schedules.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.440
A documented exception is a managed risk.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.740
An forgotten exception becomes drift.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.120
Change control supports safe improvement.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.580
It gives teams a shared record of what changed, why it changed, who approved it,

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.200
how it was tested, how it can be reversed,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.100
and what should be reviewed later.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.200
Hardening programs earn trust when they improve security while respecting availability and

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.380
recovery.
